Relevant tables / columns, and why we will look at them  
• veg_loss_rate_df."item_code":           unique identifier for every SKU whose loss rate we track  
• veg_loss_rate_df."item_name":           human‑readable item label, useful for reporting  
• veg_loss_rate_df."loss_rate_%":         % shrinkage we must summarise  
• veg_cat."category_name":                optional category label if we later want to split the summary (not strictly needed for the core request)

Typical conditions that may be used  
• WHERE v."loss_rate_%" < (SELECT AVG("loss_rate_%") …)     → “below average” items  
• WHERE v."loss_rate_%" > ( SELECT AVG("loss_rate_%") …)    → “above average” items  
• WHERE v."loss_rate_%" BETWEEN avg_lr‑std_dev AND avg_lr+std_dev   → “within one std‑dev”  

Below are ten exploratory SQL queries that gradually build from simple inspection to the final one that returns the requested summary.  
(Every query uses SELECT … DISTINCT … and LIMIT 20 so you can run them safely in SQLite.)

```sql
-- 1. See a sample list of distinct vegetable names we hold loss‑rate data for
SELECT DISTINCT v."item_name"
FROM   veg_loss_rate_df AS v
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Inspect a few distinct item codes
SELECT DISTINCT v."item_code"
FROM   veg_loss_rate_df AS v
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Glance at the different loss‑rate percentages that appear
SELECT DISTINCT v."loss_rate_%"
FROM   veg_loss_rate_df AS v
ORDER  BY v."loss_rate_%" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Calculate the overall average loss‑rate (%)
SELECT DISTINCT ROUND(AVG(v."loss_rate_%"),2) AS avg_loss_rate_pct
FROM   veg_loss_rate_df AS v
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. List a few items whose loss‑rate is BELOW that overall average
SELECT DISTINCT v."item_code",
       v."item_name",
       v."loss_rate_%"
FROM   veg_loss_rate_df AS v,
       (SELECT AVG("loss_rate_%") AS avg_lr FROM veg_loss_rate_df) AS a
WHERE  v."loss_rate_%" < a.avg_lr
ORDER  BY v."loss_rate_%" ASC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. List a few items whose loss‑rate is ABOVE the overall average
SELECT DISTINCT v."item_code",
       v."item_name",
       v."loss_rate_%"
FROM   veg_loss_rate_df AS v,
       (SELECT AVG("loss_rate_%") AS avg_lr FROM veg_loss_rate_df) AS a
WHERE  v."loss_rate_%" > a.avg_lr
ORDER  BY v."loss_rate_%" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Work out the population standard deviation of the loss‑rate %
SELECT DISTINCT 
       ROUND(
           SQRT( AVG( v."loss_rate_%" * v."loss_rate_%") 
                 - AVG(v."loss_rate_%") * AVG(v."loss_rate_%") ), 2
       ) AS std_dev_loss_rate_pct
FROM   veg_loss_rate_df AS v
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Show a few items whose loss‑rate lies WITHIN ±1 standard deviation
WITH stats AS (
    SELECT AVG("loss_rate_%") AS avg_lr,
           SQRT( AVG("loss_rate_%":"loss_rate_%") - AVG("loss_rate_%")*AVG("loss_rate_%")) AS std_lr
    FROM   veg_loss_rate_df
)
SELECT DISTINCT v."item_code",
       v."item_name",
       v."loss_rate_%"
FROM   veg_loss_rate_df AS v, stats
WHERE  v."loss_rate_%" BETWEEN (stats.avg_lr - stats.std_lr)
                           AND (stats.avg_lr + stats.std_lr)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. OPTIONAL: join to veg_cat to see categories for some of those in query 8
WITH stats AS (
    SELECT AVG("loss_rate_%") AS avg_lr,
           SQRT( AVG("loss_rate_%":"loss_rate_%") - AVG("loss_rate_%")*AVG("loss_rate_%")) AS std_lr
    FROM   veg_loss_rate_df
)
SELECT DISTINCT v."item_code",
       v."item_name",
       c."category_name",
       v."loss_rate_%"
FROM   veg_loss_rate_df AS v
LEFT   JOIN veg_cat          AS c ON v."item_code" = c."item_code",
       stats
WHERE  v."loss_rate_%" BETWEEN (stats.avg_lr - stats.std_lr)
                           AND (stats.avg_lr + stats.std_lr)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL answer: average loss‑rate, plus counts of items that are
--     below, within, and above one standard deviation of that average
SELECT DISTINCT
       ROUND(stats.avg_lr,2)                       AS avg_loss_rate_pct,
       ROUND(stats.std_lr,2)                       AS std_dev_pct,
       SUM( CASE WHEN v."loss_rate_%" <  stats.avg_lr - stats.std_lr THEN 1 END )  AS cnt_below_1std,
       SUM( CASE WHEN v."loss_rate_%" BETWEEN stats.avg_lr - stats.std_lr 
                                          AND   stats.avg_lr + stats.std_lr THEN 1 END ) AS cnt_within_1std,
       SUM( CASE WHEN v."loss_rate_%" >  stats.avg_lr + stats.std_lr THEN 1 END )  AS cnt_above_1std
FROM   veg_loss_rate_df AS v
CROSS  JOIN (
        SELECT AVG("loss_rate_%")                                                    AS avg_lr,
               SQRT( AVG("loss_rate_%":"loss_rate_%") - AVG("loss_rate_%")*AVG("loss_rate_%") ) AS std_lr
        FROM   veg_loss_rate_df
       ) AS stats
LIMIT 20;
```

Run the last query to obtain the requested summary:  
• average loss‑rate (%) across all tracked items  
• how many items fall more than 1 σ below that mean  
• how many sit inside ±1 σ (i.e. typical performers)  
• how many are more than 1 σ above the mean (high‑loss items)
